<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tinian</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tinian is one of the three main islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a> in the western Pacific, just south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>. Under Japanese rule it was covered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations. Captured by US forces in July and August 1944, it became one of the largest airbases in the world, from which the B-29 bombers that dropped the atomic bombs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> took off in August 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tinian.jpg" alt="Tinian" width="290" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: map:US Geological Survey crop:Wikid77 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Saipan_Tinian_islands_closer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tinian is one of the three principal islands of the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a>. It lies about 9.3 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saipan">Saipan</a>, across the Saipan Channel, and with the uninhabited island of Aguijan forms Tinian Municipality. The island has a land area of about 100 square kilometres and is much flatter than Saipan, with limestone cliffs and caves and a highest point of 187 metres on the Kastiyu plateau. Its main village, San Jose, is on the south-west coast, and the Tinian monarch is the island's only endemic bird.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> were the first islands settled by humans in Remote Oceania, around 1500 to 1400 BCE, by migrants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. A people who built stone structures called taga lived on Tinian thousands of years ago. The Spanish formally occupied the island in 1669 with the missionary expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-luis-de-san-vitores">Diego Luis de San Vitores</a>, who named it Buenavista Mariana.</p><p>The native population, estimated at 40,000 when the Spanish arrived, fell to under 1,400 through introduced diseases and conflicts over land, and the survivors were forcibly moved to Guam in 1720. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sold the island to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured it in 1914 and was given formal control in 1918 under the South Seas Mandate, and settlers from Japan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and Okinawa built large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations; by June 1944 about 15,700 Japanese civilians lived there.</p><p>The Japanese did not garrison Tinian until the late stages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when its value as a base for American B-29 bombers became clear. Allied forces took the island in the Battle of Tinian, from 24 July to 1 August 1944, in which only 313 of the 8,500-man Japanese garrison survived and many hundreds of civilians were killed or took their own lives. The Americans then built the largest airbase of the war, with a naval base for 40,000 personnel. From North Field the B-29s Enola Gay and Bockscar took off carrying the atomic bombs dropped on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The loading pits for the atomic bombs and the remains of the bomber base and Japanese fortifications can still be seen at North Field, where a memorial stands over the pits. After the war Tinian became part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands and has been a municipality of the Commonwealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-mariana-islands">Northern Mariana Islands</a> since 1978. The House of Taga, a site of latte stones quarried from limestone, is one of the most significant structures in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a>, although only one of its twelve large structures still stands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tinian, Northern Mariana Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan developed sugar plantations on Saipan and <b>Tinian</b> through the South Seas Development Company, phosphate mining and fisheries.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li></ul></section>
